<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Rapallo (1920)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1920</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Rapallo was signed on 12 November 1920 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> to settle their border after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Italy received Istria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a> (Zadar) and several islands, while the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> went to the Yugoslav kingdom, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> became the Free State of Fiume. Italian troops ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">Gabriele D'Annunzio</a>'s regime in Fiume the following month.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rapallo-1920.jpg" alt="Treaty of Rapallo (1920)" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:43Trattato_Rapallo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
